1.13.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A "Draft" button in the category editor: the shop assembles a starting text out of what it already knows — what the category holds, how many items, the price range in your currency and your own delivery terms. Nothing is invented and nothing is published; it lands in the box to be rewritten. An empty box is the reason category texts never get written
- Deleting a category text is its own button and its own endpoint, instead of hiding behind "save an empty field"
- A text of your own on a category page, written in the new Categories tab: it shows above the goods and becomes the page description for search engines. A listing plus a paragraph is the landing page shops keep a separate site section for; without it every category page describes itself with the same generated sentence
- The Categories tab lists the whole tree with the number of goods in each node and shows at a glance which ones are still without a text

1.12.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A page for every category: /c/tekstil/spalnya/kpb-evro with its own title, description, breadcrumbs and listing. A search for "купить КПБ евро оптом" lands on a page about that, not on the catalogue with a query parameter; the old ?category= address redirects to the new one permanently
- Categories now arrive with the catalogue. Every source fills them: the tree of a YML feed, the Ozon taxonomy (one request per import, not one per card), the WB subject directory, and a price-list cell written as "Текстиль > Спальня". Until now the column existed and no importer ever wrote to it — a shop of 20,000 products had one category page, empty
- A category is a path, so a parent shows everything below it: "Текстиль" lists the goods of "Текстиль/Спальня/КПБ", which gives the shop landing pages for broad searches as well as narrow ones
- Category pages and the category index are in the sitemap, each with its own lastmod
- Filters for the buyer, still without a line of JavaScript: sort by price or name, "in stock only". The state lives in the address, so a filtered list can be sent to someone; every variant points a canonical at the plain page, so search engines see one page and not five
- HEAD returned 405 for every storefront page: a GET-only route on the outer router answered before the storefront could. Monitoring, link checkers and some crawlers saw a shop that looked broken

1.11.0 / August 14, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Legal details for the shop: a free-form block in the Profile that goes into the footer of every storefront page and into schema.org/Organization markup, so search engines and buyers can see whom they are dealing with
- A "Delivery and payment" page: delivery, payment and returns written as plain text in the Profile, published at /info, linked from the footer and listed in the sitemap. Neither Yandex nor Google accepts a shop into shopping results without published terms; an empty field means no page and no link
- Product feeds for Yandex (/yml.xml) and Google Merchant Center (/gmc.xml)
- Search engines and analytics: Google Analytics and Yandex Metrika ids in the Profile, and the storefront still carries no script at all while both are empty
- Order emails come from the shop, not from a mailbox: the sender name is the shop name, and the address can differ from the SMTP login for owners whose provider forbids sending as another user
- A product with no photos shows a placeholder instead of an empty column half a page tall
- Instance stats in the admin: CPU, memory, disks, network, uptime, the size of the database and of the uploads directory
- New shops are handed a one-time invite link instead of a printed password
- An uploaded logo larger than needed is shrunk on upload
- The storefront's html lang follows the shop language
- Search-engine verification fields are gone from the Profile: proving domain ownership is the owner's business, and every registrar and search engine offers a way that needs no field in the shop
- The admin keeps one page width across tabs, and a product dialog scrolls itself instead of the page behind it

1.10.0 / August 10, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The catalogue grid shows small copies of the photos instead of the supplier's originals: a page of sixty products used to pull megabytes to draw thumbnails. Measured on a real catalogue, a 197 KB photo becomes 16 KB. The product page still shows the original
- Photos already in a shop get their small copies from the second stage of "Fill in", with the same progress and the same stop button
- Uploaded files are served with a long, immutable cache: a repeat visitor downloads no photos at all
- The admin panel had the storefront's icon in its browser tab — the seller's initial, generated for their shop — because it asked for /favicon.svg by absolute path

